      <h3>Site Preparation for African Conditions</h3>
      <p>African mining sites often present unique challenges. Based on installations in the DRC, Mozambique, and Zimbabwe:</p>
      
      <p><strong>Site Access:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Road conditions:</strong> Plan for poor roads, especially during rainy season</li>
        <li><strong>Bridge capacity:</strong> Many rural bridges can't handle fully loaded container trucks</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonal access:</strong> Some sites become inaccessible during heavy rains</li>
        <li><strong>Alternative transport:</strong> Consider smaller trucks or off-loading containers near good roads</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>Site Preparation:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Level pad:</strong> 100' x 60' minimum for M50, larger for bigger units</li>
        <li><strong>Drainage:</strong> Critical in tropical climates — plan for 6+ months of rain</li>
        <li><strong>Equipment access:</strong> Plan paths for excavators and trucks</li>
        <li><strong>Storage area:</strong> Covered storage for spare parts and tools</li>
        <li><strong>Fuel storage:</strong> Proper storage for diesel fuel (often expensive/scarce)</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Power and Water Infrastructure</h3>
      <p>Most African mining sites lack grid power and municipal water. Here's what works:</p>
      
      <p><strong>Power Solutions:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Generator power:</strong> Most common — plan for 125% of rated wash plant power</li>
        <li><strong>Fuel consumption:</strong> Budget 3-5 gallons diesel per hour for M50</li>
        <li><strong>Solar hybrid:</strong> Emerging option, especially for water pumping</li>
        <li><strong>Backup power:</strong> Always have backup generator — downtime is expensive</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>Water Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>M50:</strong> 500 GPM through the plant, far less hauled once you recirculate</li>
        <li><strong>M100:</strong> 1,200 GPM through the plant, far less hauled once you recirculate</li>
        <li><strong>Source options:</strong> River, borehole, or constructed pond</li>
        <li><strong>Pumping power:</strong> Factor in additional generator capacity for water pumps</li>
        <li><strong>Backup water:</strong> Dry season contingency planning essential</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Shipping to African Ports</h3>
      <p>Shipping logistics vary significantly by African port:</p>
      
      <p><strong>West Africa Ports:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lagos, Nigeria:</strong> Good handling but congested (allow extra time)</li>
        <li><strong>Tema, Ghana:</strong> Excellent mining equipment experience</li>
        <li><strong>Abidjan, Côte d'Ivoire:</strong> Good regional hub for West African distribution</li>
        <li><strong>Customs:</strong> Varies by country — use experienced local brokers</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>East Africa Ports:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dar es Salaam, Tanzania:</strong> Major mining equipment hub</li>
        <li><strong>Mombasa, Kenya:</strong> Good for East African distribution</li>
        <li><strong>Maputo, Mozambique:</strong> Growing capacity, good for southern routes</li>
        <li><strong>Inland transport:</strong> Plan carefully — distances can be substantial</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>Southern Africa:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Durban, South Africa:</strong> Best infrastructure, excellent for regional distribution</li>
        <li><strong>Port Elizabeth:</strong> Good alternative with shorter inland distances</li>
        <li><strong>Beira, Mozambique:</strong> Direct access to inland countries</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Country-Specific Considerations</h3>
      <p><strong>Democratic Republic of Congo:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rich alluvial deposits throughout the country</li>
        <li>Infrastructure challenges — plan for self-sufficiency</li>
        <li>Import procedures can be complex — use experienced customs brokers</li>
        <li>M50 most popular due to transportability</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>Zimbabwe:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Both alluvial and hard rock mining opportunities</li>
        <li>Better infrastructure than many African countries</li>
        <li>Established mining industry with skilled operators</li>
        <li>M100 and M200 models gain popularity</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p><strong>Mozambique:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Coastal alluvial deposits and inland hard rock</li>
        <li>Improving infrastructure but still developing</li>
        <li>Portuguese language — consider operator training materials</li>
        <li>Growing market for all wash plant sizes</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Local Support and Training</h3>
      <p>Success in Africa requires local capability building:</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Operator training:</strong> Essential — African operators are eager to learn</li>
        <li><strong>Local mechanics:</strong> Train local technicians for routine maintenance</li>
        <li><strong>Spare parts inventory:</strong> Stock critical parts locally — shipping delays are common</li>
        <li><strong>Language considerations:</strong> French, Portuguese, English — prepare materials accordingly</li>
        <li><strong>Cultural sensitivity:</strong> Respect local practices and build community relationships</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Economic Considerations</h3>
      <p><strong>Total Cost of Ownership:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Equipment cost:</strong> 40-50% of total investment</li>
        <li><strong>Shipping:</strong> 10-15% for single container units</li>
        <li><strong>Site preparation:</strong> 15-20% depending on remoteness</li>
        <li><strong>Power infrastructure:</strong> 10-15% (generators, fuel storage)</li>
        <li><strong>Water infrastructure:</strong> 5-10% (pumps, storage, distribution)</li>
        <li><strong>Working capital:</strong> 6 months operating expenses minimum</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Success Factors for African Mining</h3>
      <p>Based on successful operations across Africa:</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Start smaller:</strong> Prove deposits before investing in larger equipment</li>
        <li><strong>Plan for self-sufficiency:</strong> Generator power, spare parts, fuel storage</li>
        <li><strong>Build local relationships:</strong> Community support is essential</li>
        <li><strong>Train extensively:</strong> Invest in operator and maintenance training</li>
        <li><strong>Plan for wet season:</strong> 4-6 months of difficult conditions annually</li>
        <li><strong>Quality partnerships:</strong> Work with experienced equipment suppliers and logistics providers</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Regional Mining Trends</h3>
      <p>African gold mining is evolving rapidly:</p>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Small-scale mechanization:</strong> Transition from artisanal to mechanized methods</li>
        <li><strong>Environmental awareness:</strong> Growing focus on responsible mining practices</li>
        <li><strong>Infrastructure development:</strong> Improving roads, power, and communications</li>
        <li><strong>Skills development:</strong> Growing technical competence across the continent</li>
        <li><strong>Regional cooperation:</strong> Cross-border projects and shared expertise</li>
      </ul>
      
